Correctness and API safety:
- matrix2/3/4_invert leave the matrix unchanged when it has no inverse (see matrix4_invert);
  consider returning a status the caller can check, and document the behavior
- Improve the "has an inverse" check in matrix2/3/4_inverse. It currently uses
  scalar_equalsf(determinant, 0), i.e. |det| < HYP_EPSILON. Because the determinant
  scales with the cube/fourth power of the matrix scale, very small uniform scales are
  treated as having no inverse: in the float build, uniform scales below ~0.056 (e.g.
  0.01, det 1e-6); in double, below ~0.001.
  Findings:
  - An exact-zero check (det == 0) catches structured singular matrices (a zero scale,
    a row that is an exact multiple of another), but rounding can leave a small nonzero
    determinant: in a test of 1000 singular matrices with one row = sum of two others,
    ~80% had a determinant of ~1e-8 (float) / ~1e-18 (double). So exact zero alone is
    not enough.
  - Any threshold on the determinant alone has this trade-off.
  - Recommended: scale-independent ratio (Hadamard's inequality). ratio =
    |det| / product of row lengths is in [0, 1]; do the same with columns and take the
    larger; "no inverse" if it is below HYP_EPSILON. Rows alone or columns alone depend
    on the order transforms were combined (rotate * scale(1000, 0.001) has row ratio 1,
    column ratio 2e-6, and inverts exactly). Tested: small uniform scales and
    rotate/scale in either order invert; 100000 rounded singular matrices were all
    rejected (worst ratio 1.2e-7 float, 2.5e-16 double). It is a rule of thumb: in float
    it also declines rotate * scale(1000, 0.001) * rotate, which float precision cannot
    invert accurately (~3% error).
    Document as "returns NULL if the matrix is not invertible, or too close to it to
    invert accurately at this precision".
  - LAPACK's approach: report singular only on an exact zero pivot and provide a
    separate condition estimate. Could add a matrixN condition-estimate function later.
- The same "within HYP_EPSILON of zero" guard is used for vector normalize, quaternion
  normalize / inverse, quaternion_is_pure and the SLERP shortcuts; review each the same way
- Answer the SLERP shortest-arc question above with a test, then fix if needed
- Test quaternion inverse with non-unit quaternions (see above) and fix if needed
- HYP_EPSILON is 1E-5 (single) and 1E-12 (double), compared as an absolute difference.
  Consider relative / ULP-based comparisons for large magnitudes (e.g. the matrix6
  single precision inverse lands at ~2.3e-5)

C89 compliance:
- `gcc -std=c89 -pedantic` reports 32 warnings: 25 unnamed structs/unions, 7 structs with
  no named members. Decided: the anonymous unions stay; the README says "almost 100%
  C89/C90 compliant".
- <stdint.h> / uint8_t are C99

